<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-7-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Eigenvalues </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Compute eigenvalues of a matrix. </p></li>
            <li><p> Find eigenvectors associated with eigenvalues. </p></li>
            <li><p> Construct generalized eigenvectors for eigenvalues with higher multiplicity. </p></li> 
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-identity">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We call <m>\cal{I}_{n \times n}</m> the <m>n \times n</m> <alert>identity matrix</alert> where <md> \cal{I} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; \cdots &amp; 0 \\ \vdots &amp; \vdots &amp; \ddots &amp; \vdots \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}. </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute <m>AB</m> where <md> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 2 \\ -4 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\ 2 &amp; 6 &amp; -2 \end{bmatrix} \ \text{and} \ B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\ 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 5 \\ 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}. </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    

    <definition xml:id="def-eigenvalues">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>\xi \in \real^n</m>. We call <m>\lambda \in \real</m> an <alert>eigenvalue</alert> of <m>A</m> provided <md> A\xi = \lambda \xi.</md> We call <m>\xi</m> the <alert>eigenvector</alert> corresponding to <m>\lambda</m>. Together, <m>(\lambda, \xi)</m> form an <alert>eigenpair</alert> for <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    
    <p>
        Notice that we can write 
        <md>
            <mrow> A\xi \amp= \lambda \xi \implies </mrow> 
            <mrow> A\xi - \lambda \xi \amp= 0 \implies </mrow>
            <mrow> (A - \lambda \cal{I})\xi \amp= 0 </mrow>
        </md>
        Results from linear algebra then show that <m>\det(A - \lambda \cal{I}) = 0</m>. This is how we will find eigenvalues.         
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all eigenvalues for the matrix <md> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\ 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix}.</md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Eigenvalues are the roots to the <alert>characteristic polynomial</alert> for the matrix. We compute 

                <md>
                    A - \lambda \cal{I}_{3 \times 3} = \begin{vmatrix} 1 - \lambda &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\ 0 &amp; -1-\lambda &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 3-\lambda \end{vmatrix} = 0.
                </md>
                This shows that <m>\lambda = 1,-1,3</m>. 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all eigenvectors for the matrix <md> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\ 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix}.</md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Recall that <m>\lambda=1,-1,3</m> are eigenvalues for <m>A</m>. Also recall that eigenvectors <m>\bf{\xi}</m> satisfy <md> (A - \lambda \cal{I}) \bf{\xi} = 0 </md>. So we solve 
                
                <md>
                    \lambda = 1 \implies \begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\ 0 &amp; -2 &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} \xi_1 \\ \xi_2 \\ \xi_3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
                
                Repeating for each eigenvalues gives <md>\xi_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \xi_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \xi_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}. </md> 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
        Sometimes, eigenvalues have higher <alert>multiplicity</alert>. When this happens, we don't quite have enough eigenvectors to go around, so we need to compute <alert> generalized eigenvectors</alert>. The procedure is as follows. 
    </p>
 
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all eigenvectors for the matrix <md> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix}.</md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</section>
